// ============================================================================
// hello.ag — The Agape Language, taught top to bottom.
//
// Read this file from the first line to the last. Each concept is introduced
// before it is used. By the end you will understand every primitive in the
// language. Agape is a language for building multi-agent systems where the
// agents are first-class, cognition is a swappable substrate, and *meaning*
// is something the program can check — not just hope for.
//
// Two ideas sit under everything else:
//
//   1. THE EVENT SPINE. Every meaningful action appends an immutable event to
//      a single append-only log. The log is the source of truth. State is a
//      projection of the log; replay re-derives state from it. Nothing that
//      matters happens off the spine.
//
//   2. THE PROVIDER SEAM. Whenever the program needs cognition — to generate a
//      response or to embed text — it calls through a seam (`think`/`embed`)
//      that the program never names directly. Swap the provider (a mock, the
//      Anthropic API, a local model) and not one line of Agape changes.
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// PART 1 — VALUES, TYPES, AND THE TWO COLORS OF CODE
// ============================================================================

// Agape has ordinary scalar types: bool, int, float, text, and null.
// Literals and arithmetic work as you'd expect.

int    a         = 2 + 3;          // 5
float  ratio     = 7.0 / 2.0;      // 3.5
bool   t         = true;
text   greet     = "hello";
text   formatted = f"a={a}, r={ratio}";   // f-strings interpolate with {expr}

// Functions & Routines
//
//   A function is synchronous. It may NOT touch the
//   provider seam (no `<-`, no `~`, no `entail`, no `embed`). The compiler
//   rejects any `fn` whose body reaches the seam. If you can read a `fn`,
//   you KNOW it never calls a model.
//
//   Add a `async` keyword to turn a function into a ROUTINE (asynchronous). It
//   MAY touch the seam. It has a pending window: it emits a Started event when
//   it begins and a Resolved event when it completes (see the spine, Part 3).
//   Anything that depends on a routine's result is itself a routine — async color
//   is contagious, and the compiler enforces the contagion upward.

int double(int x) {
    return x * 2;               // pure arithmetic
}

// `is_about_poker` reaches the seam (`~` calls embed), so it MUST be `async`.
// Declaring it without `async` would be a compile error: "sync function cannot touch cognition."
bool async is_about_poker(text x) {
    return x ~ "a game of poker";   // `~` is a semantic check — see Part 4
}


// ============================================================================
// PART 2 — AGENTS: TEMPLATES, INSTANCES, AND LIFECYCLE
// ============================================================================

// `agent` declares a TEMPLATE — a reusable kind of agent, like a class.
// An agent has its own private memory (Part 6) and its own mailbox.
//
// LIFECYCLE — every agent moves through these states, and each transition is
// an event on the spine:
//
//   spawn  — create a named instance. The constructor body runs.
//   awake  — open the mailbox. Queued sends now process.
//   sleep  — close the mailbox. New sends queue until the next awake.
//            A sleeping agent with no live references is garbage-collected,
//            and its release runs the `when (sleep self)` block.

// `self` is the agent's reference to itself.
// `<-` is the ONE communication operator: `destination <- message` sends a
// message into an agent and (when a typed result is bound) waits for the typed
// reply. There is exactly one send operator; there is no second arrow. Sending
// into the seam is how an agent "thinks."

agent NamedAgent(text n) {

    // A field. `event<T>` is a typed slot whose value arrives from cognition or
    // from another agent. Here it will hold the verdict of a name check.
    event<Verification> name_verification;

    // The constructor body runs at spawn. This send seeds the agent's identity.
    // `event<null>` means "we send, but expect no reply" — fire-and-forget.
    event<null> init = self <- f"Your name is {n}.";

    // `when (X)` is a SUBSCRIPTION to the spine, keyed by subject X. It fires
    // when X resolves. It is NOT a callback you wire up by hand — it is a
    // standing subscription over the log, so it works retroactively (an event
    // already in the log) and prospectively (an event yet to come).
    when (init) {
        // Ask the agent its name. `event<text>` constrains the reply to text;
        // the seam is told to produce text via structured output (Part 5).
        event<text> reply = self <- "What is your name?";

        // `verify ... ~ ...` asserts semantic closeness (Part 4). On failure it
        // emits FailedVerification(reply) to the spine; a `catch` elsewhere can
        // react. On success it emits SuccessfulVerification(reply).
        verify reply ~ f"My name is {n}.";

        // Store the verdict in our field (use `Verification` parent type as we
        // don't know if it is successful or failed), and announce progress with
        // a custom event. `emit` appends a user-defined event to the spine.
        self.name_verification = Verification(reply);
        emit Event(f"NamedAgent {n} validated.");
    }

    // The startup hook. Fires when this agent is awoken (but not initialized).
    on awake {
        emit Event(f"NamedAgent {n} activated.");
    }

    // The release hook. Fires when this agent is slept and collected.
    on sleep {
        emit Event(f"NamedAgent {n} released.");
    }
}

// `extend` is composition/inheritance: a Coach IS a NamedAgent plus more.
// The parent's fields, constructor, and `when` blocks all apply to the child.
agent Coach(text n) {
    extend NamedAgent(n);

    event<null> role = self <- "You are a poker coach.";

    when (role) {
        // `event<bool>` constrains the reply to a boolean. The seam returns a
        // real bool via structured output — no string parsing on our side.
        event<bool> confirmed = self <- "Are you a poker coach?";
        if (!confirmed) {
            emit Error(f"Coach {n} did not understand its role.");
        } else {
            emit Event(f"Coach {n} ready to roll!");
        }
    }
}

agent Student(text n) {
    extend NamedAgent(n);

    event<null> role = self <- "You are a poker student.";

    when (role) {
        event<bool> confirmed = self <- "Are you a poker student?";
        if (!confirmed) {
            emit Error(f"Student {n} did not understand its role.");
        } else {
            emit Event(f"Student {n} ready to roll!");
        }
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// PART 3 — THE SPINE IN ACTION: SPAWN, AWAKE, SEND
// ============================================================================
//
// Everything below is the top-level program. Each statement appends to the
// spine. Cognitive operations (anything that reaches the seam) append a PAIR:
//
//     <Op>Started   — the operation began; its result is not yet known
//     <Op>Resolved  — the operation completed; the result is now on the log
//
// "Pending" is simply a Started with no matching Resolved yet. If an agent
// crashes between the two, the spine shows exactly what it was waiting on.
// Synchronous, deterministic operations (==, arithmetic) append a single event.

spawn Coach   john("John");     // SpawnStarted/SpawnResolved for the constructor
awake john;                     // mailbox opens; agent can receive events

spawn Student mary("Mary");
awake mary;

// Teach john a fact. This send carries knowledge into john's memory, where it
// is internalized (Part 6). `event<null>` — we record the teaching, bind no reply.
event<null> taught = john <- "A flush beats a straight.";


// ============================================================================
// PART 4 — SEMANTIC CHECKING, PART ONE: SIMILARITY (`~`) AND `verify`
// ============================================================================
//
// `~` is the SIMILARITY operator. It asks: is the left value semantically close
// to the right one? It resolves by embedding both sides (through the seam) and
// comparing in the asking agent's own semantic memory. It is a routine (async).
//
//   - On a SCALAR: one similarity verdict.
//   - On an ARRAY: the check DECOMPOSES — it considers the parts — but still
//     returns ONE Verification for the whole. The verdict's evidence records
//     *where* a partial mismatch was, so the result type stays uniform.
//
// `verify EXPR` turns a check into an ASSERTION that emits to the spine:
//   pass  -> SuccessfulVerification(subject)
//   fail  -> FailedVerification(subject)
// The subject is the left operand. A `catch` keys off that subject — so the
// verify and its handler need not be lexically near each other.

event<text> Name = john <- "What is your name?";
event<bool> Rule = john <- "Does a flush beat a straight?";

verify Name ~ "John";                              // similarity assertion
verify Rule ~ "Yes, a flush beats a straight.";    // similarity assertion

// `verify ... ==` is the SYNCHRONOUS sibling: exact, deterministic equality.
// No embedding, no pending window — a single spine event. Use it when you mean
// "literally equal," not "close in meaning."
verify Name == "John";


// ============================================================================
// PART 5 — ERRORS ARE EVENTS: `catch`, `when`, AND SUBJECT KEYING
// ============================================================================
//
// `catch` and `when` are the SAME mechanism — a spine subscription keyed by
// (event type, subject) — at opposite polarities:
//
//   when (X)  fires on the SUCCESS/resolution events of subject X
//   catch ... fires on the FAILURE events of subject X
//
// Both are retroactive AND prospective: a subscription matches the most recent
// matching event already on the log at registration, then fires once per new
// match. (To sweep ALL of history, you write a query — Part 7 — not a catch.)
//
// Because these are subscriptions, "errors" are not exceptions that unwind a
// stack. They are events. The system keeps running; handlers observe.

// Catch all errors, including FailedVerification, regardless of the source/subject
catch Error as e {
    say("Error happened:");
    say(e);
}

catch FailedVerification(Name) as e {
    say("name check failed:");    // only look at the `==` verification check
    say(e);                       // `say` prints; e carries the verdict + evidence
}

catch FailedVerification(Rule) as e {
    say("rule check failed:");
    say(e);
}


// ============================================================================
// PART 6 — AGENT MEMORY: THREE MODALITIES, ONE UNIT
// ============================================================================
//
// Every agent has its OWN memory architecture — three modalities working as one:
//
//   FACTS         -> a deterministic table   (queried with `select`)
//   RELATIONSHIPS -> a graph                 (queried with `find ... where`)
//   SEMANTICS     -> a vector store          (queried with `match`, and the
//                                             substrate behind `~` and `entail`)
//
// Internalization is IMPLICIT: every event an agent receives is decomposed by
// the seam into facts, relationships, and embeddings, and written to that
// agent's memory. The agent literally learns from everything it experiences.
//
// QUERIES ARE PROGRAMMER-LEVEL INTROSPECTION. Because Agape knows every spawned
// agent at compile time, a query with no named target fans out across ALL
// agents' memories and aggregates. The runtime needs no central database — it
// knows the population of agents and asks each one.

// Graph query: traverse relationships. `find BINDING where { PATTERN }`.
// This asks every agent's graph for a Coach related by `is_named` to some n.
find n where { Coach is_named n };
verify n == "John";

find m where { Student is_named m };
verify m == "Mary";

// Bind every named agent's name into a list.
find names where { Agent is_named names };

// Fact query (the table modality). `select COLS from AGENT where { CONDS }`.
// `from john` targets one agent's fact table by its instance variable.
select first_name, source_timestamp from john where { type == "identity" };

// Vector query (the semantic modality). `match { BINDING: VECTOR } > THRESHOLD`.
// Returns memories whose embedding similarity clears the threshold.
match { m: "poker strategy" } > 0.8;


// ============================================================================
// PART 7 — PIPES, AGGREGATION, AND ASYNC FAN-OUT
// ============================================================================
//
// `|>` pipes each element of a collection into a function. When the function is
// a routine, this is a CONCURRENT FAN-OUT: every element's cognition call
// is launched together, then awaited. `all`/`any` reduce the results.
//
// `is_it_john` reaches the seam (`~`), so it is a routine. Therefore mapping it
// over `names` is async, and `everyone_john` is an async result — which means
// this whole region is routine-colored.

bool async is_it_john(text x) {
    return x ~ "John";
}

bool everyone_john = all(names |> is_it_john);   // concurrent fan-out, await all

// `verify` on a plain bool is a SYNCHRONOUS check (no embedding) that still
// produces a Verification verdict you can subscribe to.
event<Verification> ve = verify everyone_john;
catch (ve) as err  { say(err); }
when  (ve)         { say("all agents confirmed."); }


// ============================================================================
// PART 8 — SEMANTIC CHECKING, PART TWO: ENTAILMENT (`entail`) AND `case`
// ============================================================================
//
// Similarity (`~`) asks "are these close?" Entailment asks the harder, logical
// question: "does this output COMMIT to that claim?" Its answer is THREE-VALUED,
// because logical commitment has three outcomes:
//
//   Entailment    — the answer commits to the claim being TRUE
//   Contradiction — the answer commits to the claim being FALSE
//   Neutral       — the answer neither confirms nor denies (silence ≠ denial)
//
// `entail` is a routine. It returns a single `event<Entailment>` whose value is
// one of those three variants (a sum type, one schema, the enum the seam fills
// via structured output). Pending has no variant — an unresolved check has no
// outcome yet.
//
// `case` SYNCHRONOUSLY pattern-matches the resolved variant. The compiler checks
// the arms for exhaustiveness — for a safety-critical check, "you forgot the
// CONTRADICTION arm" is exactly the bug you want caught at compile time.

event<Verdict> async claims_flush_beats_straight() {
    event<text> answer = john <- "Does a flush beat a straight, and why?";
    return answer entail "A flush beats a straight.";
}

case (claims_flush_beats_straight()) as e {
    Entailment:    { say("john's reasoning is sound."); }
    Contradiction: { say("john contradicted a known rule!"); }
    default:       { say("john was non-committal."); }     // Neutral
}

// handle unexpected errors during function call
catch Error(claims_flush_beats_straight) as e { say(e); }

// MECHANISM vs POLICY. When `entail` resolves to Contradiction, the runtime ALSO
// emits a first-class `Contradiction` event to the spine — independently of any
// `case`. That lets a GLOBAL, standing subscription react to ANY contradiction,
// anywhere, retroactively — which a local `case` arm (lexically bound) cannot do.
//
// The runtime supplies the FACT (a contradiction exists). The programmer supplies
// the POLICY (what to do) and its COMPLETION signal. There is no built-in
// "handled" event — "handled" means something different in every application, so
// you define and emit your own.

catch Contradiction(john) as c {
    emit Error(f"john asserted something false: {c}");
    emit ContradictionHandled(john);     // user-defined completion event
}


// ============================================================================
// PART 9 — RETRY: BOUNDED RE-ATTEMPTS AS HONEST SUGAR
// ============================================================================
//
// `retry(n) { ... }` re-runs a block until it produces a successful verification
// (or n attempts are exhausted, then it emits the failure to the spine). The
// FIRST run is not a retry; only re-attempts count against n. Everything `retry`
// does — counting attempts, logging each one — is expressible by hand with a
// loop, a counter in the agent's own memory, and `catch`. It is sugar, not a
// hidden runtime: nothing the language does for you is something you couldn't
// write yourself.

retry(3) {
    event<bool> still_coach = john <- "Are you still a poker coach?";
    verify still_coach == true;
}

// The second form attaches a handler that runs BEFORE each re-attempt (never
// before the first), so you can adjust the prompt between tries. The prompt is
// just a variable; mutate it with ordinary string manipulation.

text q = "Name one rule of poker.";
event<text> rule = john <- q retry(3) {
    q = q + " Answer in one short sentence.";   // tightens the ask each retry
};


// ============================================================================
// PART 10 — GRACEFUL SHUTDOWN
// ============================================================================
//
// Sleeping an agent closes its mailbox and, when it is collected, fires its
// `when (sleep self)` release hook — which for NamedAgent emits a release event.
// Shutdown is therefore visible on the spine, in order, like everything else.

sleep john;
sleep mary;

// At the end of a run, the spine holds the COMPLETE, ordered, replayable history:
// every spawn, every think Started/Resolved pair, every verification verdict,
// every contradiction, every emit. State was only ever a projection of this log.
// To understand what happened — or to replay it deterministically — you read
// the spine.
